<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tennessee</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tennessee is a state in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with its capital at Nashville. Settled west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachians</a> from the 1760s, the region was ceded by North Carolina to the federal government in 1790 and admitted as the sixteenth state in 1796, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sevier">John Sevier</a> as its first governor. It was the last state to secede to the Confederacy in 1861, the scene of major Civil War battles, and the first readmitted in 1866.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tennessee.jpg" alt="Tennessee" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xfi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tennessee.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tennessee is a landlocked state that borders eight others: Kentucky and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> to the north, North Carolina to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to the south, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> and Missouri to the west. It is divided into three Grand Divisions, East, Middle and West Tennessee. The Blue Ridge Mountains along the eastern border reach some of the highest elevations in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the Cumberland Plateau has scenic valleys and waterfalls, and West Tennessee has level, fertile plains. The Tennessee River crosses the state twice and the Mississippi River forms its western border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleo-indians">Paleo-Indians</a> who arrived about 12,000 years ago, at the end of the Last Glacial Period. Archaeologists name the Archaic (8000–1000 BCE), Woodland (1000 BCE–1000 CE) and Mississippian (1000–1600 CE) periods. Squash, corn, gourds and sunflowers were first grown in Tennessee during the Woodland period, when the first mounds were built, and Mississippian peoples developed organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> and built many ceremonial structures. The state's name comes most directly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> town of Tanasi. A Spanish party under Juan Pardo met a village called Tanasqui in 1567, and Governor James Glen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> is credited with the modern spelling in the 1750s.</p><p>The Watauga Association of 1772, a frontier pact, is generally regarded as the first constitutional government west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a>. The area was first part of North Carolina and then of the Southwest Territory, and it became the 16th state on 1 June 1796. Its constitutions date from 1796, 1834 and 1870. The capital moved among Knoxville, Nashville and Murfreesboro before Nashville became the permanent capital in 1843. The nickname The Volunteer State reflects a strong tradition of military service.</p><p>Tennessee was a slave state until the Civil War and was politically divided: most of the west and middle supported the Confederacy, while the east was largely pro-Union. It was the last state to secede and the first former Confederate state readmitted to the Union. In the 20th century it moved from an agrarian society to a more diversified economy, helped by the Tennessee Valley Authority and by Oak Ridge, established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> to house the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>'s uranium enrichment facilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nashville is the capital and largest city; Memphis, Knoxville, Chattanooga and Clarksville follow. The state has 95 counties and about 7.2 million people. Great Smoky Mountains National Park, the most visited national park in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, lies in the east. Tennessee has done much to develop country, blues, rock and roll, soul and gospel music, and the Grand Ole Opry in Nashville has broadcast continuously since 1925.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tennessee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cherokee, in their own language Tsalagi, lived in towns across the southern Appalachians, in parts of present-day Georgia, <b>Tennessee</b>, North Carolina, South Carolina and Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lincoln's call for 75,000 militia followed on 15 April, and Virginia, Arkansas, <b>Tennessee</b> and North Carolina seceded between April and May.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain's claim, resting on the 16th-century expeditions, once reached over much of the present south-eastern United States, and Juan Pardo's expeditions from Santa Elena in 1566–1568 went as far as eastern <b>Tennessee</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Kentucky and West Virginia, perhaps mingling peacefully with local Adena peoples; the Copena culture in Alabama, Mississippi and <b>Tennessee</b>, named for its copper and galena; the Crab Orchard culture along the Ohio River; the Marksville culture of the Lower Mississippi; the Miller culture of the upper Tombigbee; and…</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The chroniclers of Hernando de Soto’s expedition describe chiefs borne in litters by their leading men, a woman ruler at Cofitachequi in present-day South Carolina, and the paramount chiefdom of Coosa in north-western Georgia, whose ruler commanded a string of chiefdoms from eastern <b>Tennessee</b> into Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States, President Woodrow Wilson, pressed by state campaigns and by pickets outside the White House, endorsed a federal amendment in 1918; Congress passed it in 1919, and ratification by <b>Tennessee</b> on 18 August 1920 made it law.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
